1267
00:55:01.980 --> 00:55:04.480
We had a fan question from Rachel S.

1268
00:55:04.620 --> 00:55:06.300
in Bloomington, Indiana, who said,

1269
00:55:06.400 --> 00:55:09.280
I absolutely love this scene in the conference room with all the exes.

1270
00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:13.600
I specifically love Gabe's line about wearing Aaron's button downs around the apartment.

1271
00:55:14.560 --> 00:55:17.800
I would love to know if this line was improvised or scripted.

1272
00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:18.960
And Maria B.

1273
00:55:19.120 --> 00:55:23.840
from Montreal said, I was curious how much of Gabe's dialogue was improvised in

1274
00:55:23.840 --> 00:55:26.460
this meeting between Andy, Pete, Alice, and Aaron.

1275
00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:31.120
Well, I'll tell you, Rachel and Maria, in our interview with Jake, he mentioned, first of

1276
00:55:31.120 --> 00:55:34.460
all, that he had a terrible stomach flu when they shot this scene.

1277
00:55:34.600 --> 00:55:39.800
I know, that he was literally going to his trailer and puking and then coming back to

1278
00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:40.080
set.

1279
00:55:40.280 --> 00:55:42.300
Yeah, he didn't know he could call in sick.

1280
00:55:42.420 --> 00:55:43.160
Oh, my gosh.

1281
00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:48.840
And then he also shared that so many of these lines were improvised by Zach Woods.

1282
00:55:49.380 --> 00:55:55.180
He did improvise the line about wearing Aaron's button downs because the scripted line was,

1283
00:55:55.280 --> 00:55:57.480
I still have a lock of her hair in my glove compartment.

1284
00:55:57.480 --> 00:56:00.420
So you could say we still have a relationship of sorts.

1285
00:56:00.420 --> 00:56:06.040
Yes, apparently Zach did a lot of improvising and did a lot of one-off lines, like the one

1286
00:56:06.040 --> 00:56:08.920
where he said he shaved his body as smooth as a porpoise.

1287
00:56:09.200 --> 00:56:14.440
Well, what's really amazing is how if you follow the script while you're watching the

1288
00:56:14.440 --> 00:56:20.820
scene, you see the pops that come from Zach Woods, but then the other people take it back

1289
00:56:20.820 --> 00:56:21.640
to the script.

1290
00:56:21.720 --> 00:56:21.960
Yes.

1291
00:56:21.960 --> 00:56:23.000
And it's so seamless.

1292
00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:29.580
It's brilliant, especially when I think about Colette being new on our set and she just

1293
00:56:29.580 --> 00:56:30.240
rolled with it.

1294
00:56:30.460 --> 00:56:32.840
She headlines in and around Zach's improv.

1295
00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:33.420
Yes.

1296
00:56:33.540 --> 00:56:35.020
I thought they all did a great job.

1297
00:56:35.200 --> 00:56:39.260
At the end of this conference room scene, Andy is a talking head and he says, does making

1298
00:56:39.260 --> 00:56:41.480
Aaron and Pete feel bad make him feel better?

1299
00:56:41.620 --> 00:56:42.780
Yeah, it does.

1300
00:56:43.840 --> 00:56:45.240
Oh my gosh, Andy.

1301
00:56:45.500 --> 00:56:46.700
So snarky, Andy.

1302
00:56:47.900 --> 00:56:50.020
This is Andy at his snarkiness.

1303
00:56:50.300 --> 00:56:51.560
At his snarkieSt.

1304
00:56:51.840 --> 00:56:52.660
What'd I say?

1305
00:56:52.880 --> 00:56:53.240
Ness.

1306
00:56:53.780 --> 00:56:54.160
Snarkiness.

1307
00:56:57.160 --> 00:56:58.040
Oh God.

1308
00:56:58.660 --> 00:57:00.800
I did not have enough tea this morning.

1309
00:57:01.240 --> 00:57:02.000
All right.

1310
00:57:02.260 --> 00:57:03.400
Here we go.

1311
00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:06.380
Buckle in because this scene just wrecked me.

1312
00:57:06.990 --> 00:57:12.040
Pam and Jim are back at Athlead and Pam is actually really going to share her real feelings

1313
00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:13.200
about coming to Philly.

1314
00:57:13.880 --> 00:57:13.980
Yeah.

1315
00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:17.640
She's talking about Mark and they're having a nice dinner.

1316
00:57:17.840 --> 00:57:19.340
They're eating their Chinese food.

1317
00:57:19.560 --> 00:57:20.740
It's kind of flirty.

1318
00:57:20.800 --> 00:57:22.500
They're reconnecting.

1319
00:57:22.780 --> 00:57:23.220
Yeah.

1320
00:57:23.440 --> 00:57:25.600
And then Jim sort of says, you know what?

1321
00:57:25.660 --> 00:57:26.260
It's all right.

1322
00:57:26.560 --> 00:57:28.340
The next interview will be better.

1323
00:57:28.780 --> 00:57:28.920
Yeah.

1324
00:57:29.060 --> 00:57:34.860
And that's when Pam pauses and says, yeah, I don't want there to be another interview.

1325
00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:38.020
And then she says she liked their life in Scranton.

1326
00:57:38.380 --> 00:57:42.360
And Jim is like, well, I started a business in Philly.

1327
00:57:42.840 --> 00:57:43.120
Yeah.

1328
00:57:43.260 --> 00:57:45.820
And Pam's like, I'm not sure I want this.

1329
00:57:47.850 --> 00:57:48.910
Oh boy.

1330
00:57:49.830 --> 00:57:51.830
We got a lot of fan mail about this.

1331
00:57:52.110 --> 00:57:54.410
I'm going to start with some practical stuff.

1332
00:57:54.870 --> 00:57:56.810
Fan question from Andrea F.

1333
00:57:56.970 --> 00:58:01.370
in Massachusetts and many others pointed out that in the scene

1334
00:58:01.370 --> 00:58:07.310
that precedes this one, Alice makes that dig about how Pete can't use chopsticks and that

1335
00:58:07.310 --> 00:58:08.690
shows the measure of a man.

1336
00:58:08.970 --> 00:58:13.270
And then we cut to a scene with Jim expertly using chopsticks.

1337
00:58:13.590 --> 00:58:14.630
Oh, I didn't even think of that.

1338
00:58:14.810 --> 00:58:15.930
People really liked that.

1339
00:58:16.330 --> 00:58:18.990
Then we got a fan question from Leighton R.

1340
00:58:19.150 --> 00:58:21.190
in Folsom, California, who just wanted to

1341
00:58:21.190 --> 00:58:22.850
know, how was the Chinese food?

1342
00:58:23.310 --> 00:58:24.250
Where'd you get it?

1343
00:58:25.390 --> 00:58:26.150
You know what?

1344
00:58:26.210 --> 00:58:28.810
I don't know where we got it, but I can tell you this.

1345
00:58:28.950 --> 00:58:29.950
It was delicious.

1346
00:58:30.490 --> 00:58:32.610
And it timed out perfectly with lunch.

1347
00:58:33.010 --> 00:58:36.490
We shot all of the athlete scenes on the same day.

1348
00:58:36.650 --> 00:58:41.530
We started first thing in the morning with those two scenes that happened in the first

1349
00:58:41.530 --> 00:58:44.490
part of the episode of Pam, you know, before her interview.

1350
00:58:44.730 --> 00:58:46.470
And then we had a little break.

1351
00:58:46.570 --> 00:58:49.770
And then they reset the stage for these dinner moments.

1352
00:58:50.250 --> 00:58:54.310
And so we were shooting this in the late morning until about 1 p.m.

1353
00:58:54.390 --> 00:58:57.510
I just, I remember I was very hungry and the food was very good.

1354
00:58:57.590 --> 00:58:58.330
I ate a lot of it.

1355
00:58:58.510 --> 00:59:03.530
I have noticed that your hunger really dictates how fresh your memories are about things.

1356
00:59:03.750 --> 00:59:04.990
My personal hunger.

1357
00:59:05.370 --> 00:59:05.570
Yeah.

1358
00:59:05.890 --> 00:59:06.830
Oh, you know what?

1359
00:59:06.950 --> 00:59:09.570
I really do have a lot of food memories.

1360
00:59:10.010 --> 00:59:10.370
Mm-hmm.

1361
00:59:10.650 --> 00:59:10.950
Yeah.

1362
00:59:11.190 --> 00:59:11.350
Yeah.

1363
00:59:11.770 --> 00:59:12.490
It's true.

1364
00:59:12.830 --> 00:59:14.870
I've noticed really enjoying those chocolates.

1365
00:59:15.310 --> 00:59:15.530
I know.

1366
00:59:16.270 --> 00:59:19.350
In our friendship, you'll be like, oh, I'll remember that day because

1367
00:59:19.350 --> 00:59:22.230
we had gone for a walk and I was so hungry.

1368
00:59:22.710 --> 00:59:23.070
And then.

1369
00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:25.790
That's probably true.

1370
00:59:25.810 --> 00:59:27.890
Then we found that Italian food place, remember?

1371
00:59:28.450 --> 00:59:29.730
Oh, that's probably true.

1372
00:59:30.150 --> 00:59:34.490
So I'll tell you, when we finished shooting this scene on this day,

1373
00:59:34.490 --> 00:59:38.310
we had a big visit from the Television Critics Association.

1374
00:59:38.710 --> 00:59:39.090
That's right.

1375
00:59:39.090 --> 00:59:40.370
We talked about it last week.

1376
00:59:40.370 --> 00:59:41.390
It was on the call sheet.

1377
00:59:41.490 --> 00:59:42.090
It was.

1378
00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:47.450
And we all gathered in the warehouse and Ed and Brian and Craig formed a band and they

1379
00:59:47.450 --> 00:59:52.830
played music and then we had this big Q&A session with them and Greg was there.

1380
00:59:53.410 --> 01:00:01.550
And this was all our way of kind of schmoozing the television critics about the final episodes

1381
01:00:01.550 --> 01:00:02.210
of The Office.

1382
01:00:02.230 --> 01:00:05.070
We wanted to give them kind of like the inside scoop.

1383
01:00:05.390 --> 01:00:05.950
The sneak peek.

1384
01:00:06.150 --> 01:00:06.510
Mm-hmm.

1385
01:00:06.730 --> 01:00:09.430
I don't think it hurt that Jon Favreau was there that day.

1386
01:00:09.510 --> 01:00:10.390
Probably didn't.

1387
01:00:10.570 --> 01:00:14.030
Angela, I saw on the call sheet that you were off that day, but you had to come in and

1388
01:00:14.030 --> 01:00:15.970
do your hair and makeup for the TCAs.

1389
01:00:16.010 --> 01:00:20.530
That was true for pretty much everyone because Jon and I were the only ones shooting that

1390
01:00:20.530 --> 01:00:20.770
day.

1391
01:00:20.910 --> 01:00:21.090
Yeah.

1392
01:00:21.250 --> 01:00:22.850
And I had to come in for the table read.

1393
01:00:22.990 --> 01:00:24.170
Which was after that.

1394
01:00:24.310 --> 01:00:24.450
Yeah.

1395
01:00:24.670 --> 01:00:26.290
There were a lot of moving parts that day.

1396
01:00:26.470 --> 01:00:32.690
I would like to also share with you all that in the shooting draft of Moving On, the episode

1397
01:00:33.230 --> 01:00:36.470
would have ended with this Jim and Pam final scene.

1398
01:00:36.790 --> 01:00:38.670
This would have been like the cliffhanger.

1399
01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:39.350
Yes.

1400
01:00:39.830 --> 01:00:48.370
And I am glad that it didn't because it is really sad and tense, and I'm glad we end

1401
01:00:48.370 --> 01:00:53.470
with a more uplifting moment and then also such a big tease.

1402
01:00:53.790 --> 01:00:54.370
Yes.

1403
01:00:54.630 --> 01:00:55.950
Well, let's get into it.

1404
01:00:56.070 --> 01:00:59.130
This is something that we shot again for Couples Discount.

1405
01:00:59.290 --> 01:00:59.470
Yep.

1406
01:00:59.690 --> 01:01:05.270
This would have been the cold open of Couples Discount, but it has now become the tag of Moving

1407
01:01:05.270 --> 01:01:05.610
On.

1408
01:01:05.710 --> 01:01:06.250
That's right.

1409
01:01:06.250 --> 01:01:07.870
Oscar's at his computer.

1410
01:01:07.870 --> 01:01:13.110
He's watching Hobbies of the East, and when a commercial break comes on, he goes over to

1411
01:01:13.110 --> 01:01:15.390
this chin-up bar that he has installed.

1412
01:01:15.730 --> 01:01:18.270
It looks like he was watching Women Play Marjohn.

1413
01:01:18.610 --> 01:01:18.810
Yeah.

1414
01:01:19.170 --> 01:01:22.970
And we find something out that really cracked me up, Jenna, especially with everything you

1415
01:01:22.970 --> 01:01:30.410
shared last week about your online purchases, is that Oscar, he watches too many online ads

1416
01:01:30.410 --> 01:01:31.890
and he doesn't do enough sit-ups.

1417
01:01:31.890 --> 01:01:37.410
So he bought ankle braces, which, by the way, he bought from an online ad, so he can

1418
01:01:37.410 --> 01:01:40.990
get 30 seconds of ab blasting whenever an ad pops up.

1419
01:01:41.510 --> 01:01:42.510
Ads for abs.

1420
01:01:42.510 --> 01:01:48.590
Well, this contraption is real, and Oscar did it.

1421
01:01:48.750 --> 01:01:49.090
Mm-hmm.

1422
01:01:49.290 --> 01:01:54.990
He goes over and he hooks himself in upside down and starts doing some crunches.

1423
01:01:55.510 --> 01:02:00.450
Meanwhile, Angela and Phyllis are pushing him aside to get in and out of the kitchen.

1424
01:02:00.710 --> 01:02:05.230
Yes, it was really fun to walk past him with just, like, my cup of coffee, like, excuse me.

1425
01:02:06.150 --> 01:02:11.150
Well, we got a few fan questions, one from Ivy K in Harrisonburg, Virginia, who said,

1426
01:02:11.290 --> 01:02:16.110
how long did Oscar have to hang from the sit-up contraption, and were there any safety meetings

1427
01:02:16.110 --> 01:02:16.630
involved?

1428
01:02:17.190 --> 01:02:23.030
And another one from Sage L in Kitchener, Ontario, Canada, who said, in the button with

1429
01:02:23.030 --> 01:02:25.310
Oscar, is that really him doing the sit-ups?

1430
01:02:25.710 --> 01:02:29.810
Well, we knew we would have to ask Oscar about this, and he sent in an audio clip.

1431
01:02:30.010 --> 01:02:31.110
Here's what he had to say.

1432
01:02:31.810 --> 01:02:32.670
Hi, Angela.

1433
01:02:32.810 --> 01:02:33.190
Hi, Jenna.

1434
01:02:33.550 --> 01:02:33.930
Let's see.

1435
01:02:34.090 --> 01:02:38.730
Was that me really hanging, and did we have safety meetings, and was that me doing sit-ups?

1436
01:02:39.890 --> 01:02:41.270
Yes to all of that.

1437
01:02:41.450 --> 01:02:42.890
I don't remember.

1438
01:02:43.010 --> 01:02:44.370
I remember doing the scene.

1439
01:02:45.270 --> 01:02:50.330
I don't remember exactly what happened before and after, because it was just a regular day.

1440
01:02:50.450 --> 01:02:53.310
It was a day on the set, which is a kind of a normal day.

1441
01:02:53.310 --> 01:02:54.390
It was a long time ago.

1442
01:02:54.930 --> 01:02:58.990
And so I'm sure we had a safety meeting, and they said, Oscar, do you think you can do

1443
01:02:58.990 --> 01:02:59.270
this?

1444
01:02:59.910 --> 01:03:04.910
I remember thinking, oh, this is like that scene in American Gigolo when Richard Gere

1445
01:03:05.510 --> 01:03:06.630
hangs upside down.

1446
01:03:06.830 --> 01:03:08.390
This is a movie from the 80s, I think.

1447
01:03:08.990 --> 01:03:11.510
Had that song, Call Me, with Blondie.

1448
01:03:12.110 --> 01:03:14.770
But anyway, so I'm sure they said, hey, can you do this?

1449
01:03:14.830 --> 01:03:16.230
And I said, yes.

1450
01:03:16.470 --> 01:03:18.530
I don't think there was a stunt person there.

1451
01:03:18.610 --> 01:03:20.090
They probably offered one to me.

1452
01:03:20.850 --> 01:03:25.050
So I hung, and at the beginning, it was easy to do the sit-ups.

1453
01:03:25.210 --> 01:03:26.950
As it went on, the scene, more takes.

1454
01:03:27.130 --> 01:03:28.270
You get more and more tired.

1455
01:03:29.390 --> 01:03:30.790
But I think it was okay.

1456
01:03:30.910 --> 01:03:33.690
Maybe towards the end, I needed help getting up towards the bar.

1457
01:03:34.290 --> 01:03:37.050
I think that might have happened towards the end.

1458
01:03:37.830 --> 01:03:39.850
But I remember there were a lot of funny takes.

1459
01:03:40.570 --> 01:03:44.230
My wife, personally, reminded me of the one where, because she looked at it, where Angela

1460
01:03:44.230 --> 01:03:46.990
walks by and pushes me aside like I'm a curtain hanging there.

1461
01:03:48.670 --> 01:03:50.070
But that was me.

1462
01:03:50.390 --> 01:03:53.530
There were no stunt people used for that.

1463
01:03:53.770 --> 01:03:55.030
It wasn't that dangerous.

1464
01:03:55.210 --> 01:03:59.470
It was just a weird scene that we did at the office.

1465
01:04:00.410 --> 01:04:02.990
I remember his face getting red.

1466
01:04:03.690 --> 01:04:08.970
Well, it's funny you say that, because Steve Burgess sent me the test videos.

1467
01:04:09.390 --> 01:04:13.430
And it was Tim from Construction and John from Special Effects.

1468
01:04:13.670 --> 01:04:16.450
They put in the bar, and they tested it.

1469
01:04:16.450 --> 01:04:20.490
And Tim hooked himself in, and he's hanging upside down.

1470
01:04:20.490 --> 01:04:24.170
And they're like, hey, man, is the blood rushing to your head?

1471
01:04:24.190 --> 01:04:25.330
And he's like, it is.

1472
01:04:26.070 --> 01:04:29.470
And he was like, it's pretty easy to get in and out of.

1473
01:04:29.570 --> 01:04:31.430
But it was really hard to do the crunches.

1474
01:04:31.810 --> 01:04:36.610
But, Ang, they even practiced the bit where they kind of like push him aside to be like

1475
01:04:36.610 --> 01:04:37.570
you or Phyllis.

1476
01:04:37.830 --> 01:04:40.090
So, yeah, they did an extensive teSt.

1477
01:04:40.490 --> 01:04:43.730
I did notice that Phyllis was like, I have hot coffee here.

1478
01:04:43.730 --> 01:04:48.150
But she was holding her hand around the mug, not using the handles.

1479
01:04:48.590 --> 01:04:48.730
Yes.

1480
01:04:48.870 --> 01:04:52.770
And then when she hands it to Stanley, he reacts as if it's super hot.

1481
01:04:52.910 --> 01:04:53.510
Yeah, I know.

1482
01:04:54.890 --> 01:05:00.890
Well, after all this business of hanging upside down, the camera goes back to Oscar's computer,

1483
01:05:00.890 --> 01:05:03.110
and we see an ad pop up.

1484
01:05:03.110 --> 01:05:09.170
And it says, coming this May, the office, an American workplace, 10 years in the making,

1485
01:05:09.430 --> 01:05:13.950
a look at the lives and loves of an average American small business office.

1486
01:05:14.930 --> 01:05:17.390
And that's what the episode ends with.

1487
01:05:18.090 --> 01:05:19.130
My gosh.

1488
01:05:19.590 --> 01:05:22.810
Well, I remember after that aired, all of our phones blowing up.

1489
01:05:22.950 --> 01:05:24.550
People were like, oh, my gosh.

1490
01:05:24.950 --> 01:05:25.190
Yeah.

1491
01:05:25.790 --> 01:05:30.290
Well, listen, before we go today, I wanted to read a letter that we got from Amy A.

1492
01:05:30.470 --> 01:05:30.550
in

1493
01:05:30.550 --> 01:05:31.930
Bakersfield, California.

1494
01:05:32.150 --> 01:05:35.650
It's very emotional, but I was very moved by it.

1495
01:05:35.870 --> 01:05:38.150
And I wanted to read it.

1496
01:05:38.410 --> 01:05:38.550
Yeah.

1497
01:05:38.790 --> 01:05:39.530
So here we go.

1498
01:05:40.190 --> 01:05:44.030
Angela and Jenna, I don't have a question, but just some words of gratitude.

1499
01:05:44.590 --> 01:05:50.950
On November 22, 2023, I lost my husband, Zach, suddenly at the age of 38.

1500
01:05:50.950 --> 01:05:56.030
For the next few months, I sulked and cried and couldn't really get out of bed.

1501
01:05:56.530 --> 01:06:01.590
We spent 15 years together, and I didn't know what adult life was like without him guiding

1502
01:06:01.590 --> 01:06:02.150
our ship.

1503
01:06:02.630 --> 01:06:08.470
On Valentine's Day this year, I decided to watch our office anniversary episode, Moving

1504
01:06:08.470 --> 01:06:08.830
On.

1505
01:06:09.570 --> 01:06:14.210
This was the first episode of The Office we ever watched together, and we found it by

1506
01:06:14.210 --> 01:06:21.010
accident because we were both sick February 14, 2013, when the episode first aired, and

1507
01:06:21.010 --> 01:06:23.190
all we wanted to do was have soup in bed.

1508
01:06:24.210 --> 01:06:24.810
Sorry.

1509
01:06:26.270 --> 01:06:28.630
After that episode, we were hooked.

1510
01:06:28.870 --> 01:06:33.830
We were office fans together and often quizzed each other during our lunch breaks and quoted

1511
01:06:33.830 --> 01:06:35.290
lines to each other constantly.

1512
01:06:35.910 --> 01:06:40.970
Because of his passing, I fell behind on your podcast episodes and began listening again

1513
01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:43.050
last Monday after returning to work.

1514
01:06:43.050 --> 01:06:48.030
I bawled on my drive home because your voices, your stories, and just memories of the show

1515
01:06:48.030 --> 01:06:50.050
took me right back to my time with him.

1516
01:06:50.970 --> 01:06:56.070
The days are still very hard, but having your podcast on in my home, where my husband's

1517
01:06:56.070 --> 01:06:59.610
presence is still very strong, gives me so much peace.

1518
01:07:00.770 --> 01:07:05.370
Watching The Office and remembering all of our inside jokes together makes me smile.

1519
01:07:05.710 --> 01:07:10.830
I will carry our mutual love of The Office with me forever, and I will laugh and probably

1520
01:07:10.830 --> 01:07:13.450
also cry every time I watch an episode.

1521
01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:17.750
Thank you both for bringing some laughter back into my life.

1522
01:07:17.950 --> 01:07:19.570
Zach would be grateful too.

1523
01:07:19.790 --> 01:07:21.710
With so much gratitude, Amy.

1524
01:07:28.460 --> 01:07:31.120
Amy, thank you so much for writing in.

1525
01:07:31.120 --> 01:07:38.760
And, you know, it's really just hard to put into words how much it means to us that The Office

1526
01:07:38.760 --> 01:07:43.980
as a show helps people through times like this or helps people connect or heal.

1527
01:07:45.020 --> 01:07:51.040
And then on top of it, that in any way that Jen and I are a part of that now with the podcast

1528
01:07:51.040 --> 01:07:52.100
to be there for you.

1529
01:07:53.080 --> 01:07:56.560
We just want to give you our love and we just hold you in our hearts.

1530
01:07:57.300 --> 01:07:57.780
Yeah.

1531
01:07:58.480 --> 01:08:00.220
Thanks for letting us be there with you.

1532
01:08:00.460 --> 01:08:00.780
Yeah.

1533
01:08:01.260 --> 01:08:01.860
We love you.

1534
01:08:02.380 --> 01:08:03.280
We love you.

1535
01:08:03.280 --> 01:08:04.400
We love you guys.

1536
01:08:04.620 --> 01:08:05.620
We love doing this.

1537
01:08:05.880 --> 01:08:15.420
And I do want you all to know that Creed texted us one last time since we spoke to him earlier

1538
01:08:15.420 --> 01:08:19.320
in the episode, and I wanted to read you the text he just sent us.

1539
01:08:19.460 --> 01:08:21.220
It's a beautiful poem.

1540
01:08:21.440 --> 01:08:22.220
It is.

1541
01:08:22.720 --> 01:08:23.520
Creed wrote,

1542
01:08:23.520 --> 01:08:29.460
Since this morning has been so serendipitous, I thought I'd send you my favorite cat quote

1543
01:08:29.460 --> 01:08:31.280
from Alan Watts.

1544
01:08:32.300 --> 01:08:35.780
When a cat falls out of a tree, it lets go of itself.

1545
01:08:36.080 --> 01:08:39.880
The cat becomes completely relaxed and lands lightly on the ground.

1546
01:08:40.320 --> 01:08:45.320
But if a cat were about to fall out of a tree and suddenly make up its mind that it didn't

1547
01:08:45.320 --> 01:08:51.340
want to fall, it would become tense and rigid and would be just a bag of broken bones upon

1548
01:08:51.340 --> 01:08:51.760
landing.

1549
01:08:52.060 --> 01:08:57.060
In the same way, it is the philosophy of the Tao that we are all falling off a tree at

1550
01:08:57.060 --> 01:08:58.580
every moment of our lives.

1551
01:08:58.720 --> 01:09:04.460
As a matter of fact, the moment we were born, we were kicked off a precipice and we are falling,

1552
01:09:04.620 --> 01:09:06.640
and there is nothing that can stop it.

1553
01:09:06.860 --> 01:09:11.900
So instead of living in a state of chronic tension and clinging to all sorts of things

1554
01:09:11.900 --> 01:09:17.460
that are actually falling with us because the whole world is impermanent, be like a cat.

1555
01:09:17.460 --> 01:09:18.940
I love it.

1556
01:09:19.560 --> 01:09:26.640
Well, a big thank you to Rainn Wilson, Oscar Nunez, Dave Rogers, Steve Burgess, and Creed

1557
01:09:26.640 --> 01:09:30.780
Bratton for brightening up this episode and to all of you.

1558
01:09:30.920 --> 01:09:34.340
For writing in, sending your questions, your comments, and letters.

1559
01:09:34.560 --> 01:09:35.860
We love you so much.

1560
